Phase 12, Portfolio and job preparation. One finished project beats five half-built ones. This phase packages the work, practices the system-design conversation, and prepares answers that show judgment rather than vocabulary. This phase has 3 lessons. Lesson 1: Ship the complete system. A strong capstone makes judgment visible: framing, data contract, honest evaluation, tests, reproducible training, API, monitoring plan, and known limitations. The question for this lesson: Can you show the full ML engineering loop?. The capstone is not a bigger model. It is proof that you can take a fuzzy question, make honest decisions, and ship something small that works. Scope is the skill being tested. Pick a problem where you can get real data, define a baseline, and explain the trade-offs to a human. A narrow system with a clear README, a measured evaluation, and an honest limitations section is far more convincing than a notebook with a beautiful loss curve. You will learn: Completeness is more impressive than an unexplained giant model., Trade-offs and limitations are evidence of engineering maturity., A reliable small system is a credible portfolio project.. Practice: "Ship a narrow end-to-end project with README. Watch out for: Too much scope before the baseline., Hiding failures., Presenting a score without decision context.. You are ready when: Write a problem contract., Pass quality gates., Demo and explain limitations.. Lesson 2: ML system design. Start with requirements, then data flow, model choice, serving, evaluation, failure modes, cost, and operations. The interview is a trade-off conversation. The question for this lesson: "Can you design the whole system. A system-design answer that starts with “I would use a transformer” has already skipped the interesting part. Clarify the user, the decision, the scale, the latency budget, and the cost of mistakes. Then choose the simplest architecture that meets those constraints. Draw the data path and the request path. Name the source of truth, the training trigger, the serving contract, the evaluation gate, and what happens when the model is unavailable. Trade-offs are the answer; the diagram is just evidence. You will learn: Requirements and constraints come before architecture., Separate offline training from online serving and evaluation., Every design needs a failure mode and an operating cost.. Practice: "Write a 45-minute design doc for a RAG support assistant: users. Watch out for: Jumping to a model before clarifying the problem., Ignoring data freshness and label delay., Drawing boxes with no latency or failure budget.. You are ready when: State requirements and constraints., Draw data and request paths., Name failure modes and cost.. Lesson 3: Interview preparation. Prepare project stories, ML fundamentals, system design, and behavioral answers. Interviewers are testing reasoning, trade-offs, and communication. The question for this lesson: Can you explain your work and your judgment under pressure?. An interview is not a trivia contest. It is a sample of how you think when you are unsure. Strong candidates clarify the question, state assumptions, compare options, and explain what they would measure. Prepare a few deep project stories rather than a list of technologies. For each one, know the problem, baseline, data, metric, failure, trade-off, and what you would do differently. Then practice saying it in two minutes and in ten. You will learn: Depth on one project beats shallow mentions of ten., State assumptions and trade-offs before details., Behavioral answers should show decisions, not just outcomes.. Practice: "Record three STAR stories and answer five system-design prompts aloud. Review for clarity. Watch out for: Reciting definitions without connecting them to a decision., Claiming a result with no baseline or measurement., Blaming teammates or tools instead of explaining the trade-off.. You are ready when: Prepare three stories., Practice one design prompt., Prepare questions to ask.. A portfolio project, a system-design walkthrough, and a practiced interview story. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
